            new("Understand the opportunity", "Compare the employer's stated qualification, graduation batch, location and experience requirements with your circumstances. Note what is essential and what is described as desirable. Check whether the position is employment, an apprenticeship or an internship, and ask about supervision and the initial learning period. If training terms, working hours or compensation are unclear, write down questions for the recruiter. Keep the vacancy link and job identifier in your application tracker so you can revisit the original information when an interview invitation arrives. Choose roles you can explain a genuine interest in."),
            new("Build a truthful application", "Use a clear resume that explains your education, relevant skills and practical work. For a college project, state the problem, your contribution, the tools you used and what you learned from testing it. A small completed project can support a stronger conversation than a long list of tools you cannot explain. Check contact details, dates and links before submitting. Give files sensible names and follow the requested format. Keep a copy of the resume you sent so your interview answers match the application. Describe achievements accurately without inventing results or experience."),
            new("Prepare for the interview", "Practise a short introduction covering your education, one relevant project and why the role interests you. Prepare examples of learning something difficult, working with others and correcting a mistake. Structure each example around the situation, your action and the result, including what you would improve. Revise the fundamentals mentioned in the vacancy and explain them aloud in simple language. Ask a friend to challenge your project claims with follow-up questions. Practise listening to the complete question before deciding which example best answers it. Review difficult questions after every practice session."),
            new("Behave professionally on the day", "Arrive or connect a little before the agreed time, follow the venue instructions and keep your phone silent. Choose clean, comfortable clothing appropriate to the setting. Greet the interviewers politely and listen without interrupting. It is acceptable to pause, ask for clarification or say that you have not encountered a topic yet. Explain how you would approach learning it rather than guessing confidently. Speak respectfully about classmates and previous teams, share credit for group work and describe your own responsibilities without exaggeration. Keep your answers focused and allow time for questions."),
            new("Ask questions and follow through", "Ask what a new joiner is expected to learn, how feedback is provided and what a typical first month involves. Clarify the next stage and the expected communication channel before leaving. If follow-up is invited, send one concise message after the stated timeline rather than repeatedly contacting several employees. Record the questions you found difficult and revise those areas for your next interview. Read any written offer carefully, confirm unclear terms through the employer's official contact and make your decision using the complete information. Continue developing skills while awaiting a response.")
